## Purpose

Harden Local Canonical correctness after the 2026-09-24 ChatGPT audit and an independent source-first local Codex cross-audit.

This issue authorizes one bounded verification-integrity implementation. It is not an optimization exercise, does not weaken existing fail-closed behavior, and does not authorize merge, Ready, auto-merge, deployment, GitHub settings changes, hosted CI, provider/model calls, or product/runtime semantics changes.

Current remote-main checkpoint when this issue is created:

`10b6a68c0fa2ce9f498c4e5eb3ce95feba743a8f`

This is a checkpoint only. Re-resolve current main before implementation and deciding verification.

## Confirmed problems

### 1. Caller-supplied deciding base is not sufficiently authenticated

Current Local Canonical verifies that supplied base/head SHAs exist and that current HEAD equals the requested head, then plans and records evidence against that supplied base/head.

The independent audit confirmed that the deciding path does not establish that the supplied base is the current integration base (for the normal PR path) or that the tested head actually contains that base before relying on the diff for responsibility selection.

This creates two distinct risks:

- an incorrectly supplied base can omit relevant earlier changes from planner classification;
- even a once-correct base can become stale relative to remote main after verification.

This slice MUST close the first/local correctness problem and make later merge-currentness checking possible. It MUST NOT claim that a non-atomic pre-merge read closes every possible GitHub merge race.

### 2. One invocation is sequential, but the checkout lacks a universal Local Canonical interprocess owner

The executor serializes phases within one invocation. That is not a checkout-wide exclusion mechanism.

The existing Companion maintenance owner protects the installed service lifecycle, but it is not proof that no second verification process can concurrently replace dependency trees, remove/recreate `.next`, or otherwise touch Local Canonical shared generated state. In particular, maintenance may legitimately return without an acquired lease when the service is absent/stopped.

A second Local Canonical process must not be able to interfere with a deciding invocation's repository-owned generated/dependency state.

## Required outcome

### A. Deciding base admission

For `changed` and `full` deciding execution:

1. Preserve exact repository/origin/head/clean-worktree checks.
2. Establish a trustworthy current integration-base observation for the canonical repository before planning long phases.
   - Prefer an existing read-only authenticated/current remote-main owner if it is already appropriate.
   - Do not create GitHub mutation authority.
   - Do not silently trust a stale local tracking ref if the existing repository has a stronger current-main read owner.
3. Require the requested base to equal the admitted current integration base for the ordinary PR verification lane.
4. Require the requested base to be an ancestor of the requested head.
5. Record enough public-safe provenance in the receipt to distinguish:
   - requested base;
   - admitted/current integration base observation;
   - ancestry result;
   - failure/refusal reason.
6. Receipt validation must reject stale/tampered or internally inconsistent integration-base evidence where the current validator can prove that condition.
7. Do not reinterpret old receipts as having this evidence.

If an existing non-PR mode genuinely needs a different base contract, preserve it explicitly rather than weakening the PR lane.

### B. Checkout-wide Local Canonical exclusion

Add a bounded, repository-owned interprocess ownership mechanism for Local Canonical operations that can touch shared verification state.

Requirements:

- independent of whether Companion is installed, live, stopped, absent, or already under maintenance;
- acquired before any deciding invocation mutates/removes/replaces shared dependency or generated state;
- deterministic refusal when a live owner exists;
- stale-owner handling must be bounded and must not kill arbitrary foreign processes;
- symlink/path escape and private-path publication remain refused;
- release occurs in bounded cleanup even after failure;
- a non-owner must never remove another invocation's lock or generated state;
- no global machine-wide lock and no new daemon/background service;
- preserve unrelated user work.

Audit whether `quick` can create or mutate shared generated state. If it can interfere with a deciding owner, coordinate that exact mutation or make Quick avoid the shared mutation. Do not over-lock read-only operations without evidence.

## Tests and evidence

Codex owns concrete implementation and test selection after inspecting current source.

At minimum add deterministic regressions for:

- correct current base admitted;
- incorrect/stale supplied base refused before deciding phases;
- base-not-ancestor-of-head refused;
- remote/current-base read unavailable fails closed without substituting a guessed base;
- integration-base provenance survives receipt finalization/validation;
- concurrent second owner refused while the first owns the checkout verification boundary;
- service absent/stopped does not disable checkout exclusion;
- cleanup releases the owner's lock after success and failure;
- stale/unsafe/symlink lock artifacts fail or recover only under an explicitly safe bounded rule;
- no non-owner generated-state deletion;
- existing single-invocation phase sequencing/cleanup remains intact.

Use source-first current tests rather than inventing a parallel framework. Reuse existing bounded process/lifecycle primitives where appropriate, but do not pretend Companion maintenance itself is the checkout lock.

## Verification

This changes Local Canonical integrity/executor/receipt/planner-adjacent behavior and therefore must remain fail-closed to the repository's current self-change qualification. Do not narrow its own deciding verification merely because this issue aims to improve the verifier.

Run focused deterministic tests first. Then run the planner-selected exact-head deciding lane required by the current policy for the final implementation head. Preserve any failed attempt truthfully.

No live provider/model calls are authorized. No GitHub Actions/hosted CI change is authorized in this slice.

## Explicit non-goals

- Do not change product/Core/protocol semantics.
- Do not change database schema or user data.
- Do not add merge authority to Codex or Local Canonical.
- Do not automatically merge or publish a PASS.
- Do not change main rulesets/settings in this slice.
- Do not solve the remaining GitHub merge-time TOCTOU race by assertion. Record it as remaining work unless an actually atomic/serialized mechanism is proven.
- Do not remove the anti-pass-fishing rule.
- Do not optimize dependency installation/resource thresholds in this slice.
- Do not redesign documentation classification here.
- Do not introduce isolated worktree verification yet.

## Delivery

Implement on a fresh branch from then-current main, preserve unrelated work, run focused checks plus required exact-head verification, and open one Draft PR.

The PR must explain:

- how the actual/current base is established;
- what base/head ancestry is required;
- what remains unproven at the final GitHub merge boundary;
- the checkout lock ownership/liveness/stale-cleanup model;
- whether Quick needed coordination and why;
- changed files and all focused/deciding results;
- any failed or skipped checks;
- no new merge/external-effect authority.

Stop at the Draft PR for ChatGPT/user review.
